    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe business-day rule for registration dates now extends to public holidays.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n\nThe controller's `toBusinessDay(...)` previously only rolled Saturdays and Sundays forward to the next weekday. I added the fixed public-holiday list and made the roll-forward loop skip holidays as well, so an adjusted date landing on a listed holiday advances to the next non-holiday business day (a holiday adjacent to a weekend chains correctly through both):\n\n```java\n/** Fixed public holidays the business-day roll skips. */\nprivate static final Set<LocalDate> HOLIDAYS = Set.of(\n    LocalDate.parse(\"2026-01-01\"), LocalDate.parse(\"2026-01-26\"),\n    LocalDate.parse(\"2026-04-25\"), LocalDate.parse(\"2026-12-25\"),\n    LocalDate.parse(\"2026-12-28\"));\n\nprivate static LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate date) {\n    while (date.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY\n        || date.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY\n        || HOLIDAYS.contains(date)) {\n        date = date.plusDays(1);\n    }\n    return date;\n}\n```\n\nThis matches the holiday list and roll logic already pinned in the test helper (`AcceptanceBase.HOLIDAYS` / `toBusinessDay`). All derived values (membership-number year segment, per-day create limit, bulk-signup flag) already flow from the adjusted date, so they inherit the new behavior automatically.\n\n**Tests**: `BUILD SUCCESS` \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0` (`AcceptanceTest`, the only test class in the project).",
